course: dictionary
lesson: 7050
title: Keeping your password secret
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A password is a secret word, or a set of letters, numbers and symbols, that proves you are allowed in. You type it to use a computer, app or account. Some places use a password too, like a secret den or club.
explain: «But her family had a password — a secret word only family and trusted friends know.» Only the people who know the password can get in, so keeping it secret keeps your account safe.
explain: «Real game companies never ask for your password on another website or in chat.» If someone asks for your password, stop and tell a parent or carer. Only share it with them.
explain: Make your password long and hard to guess, so it is not your name, your birthday or 123456. Use a different password for each account. Capital letters count, so "Cat123" and "cat123" are different passwords.
fact: A password is a secret word or set of characters you type to use a computer, app or account.
fact: A password can also be a secret word you say to be let into a place, like a den or club.
fact: Never share your password with friends. Only a parent or carer should know it.
fact: If you think someone knows your password, change it and tell an adult.
fact: A sticky note on a screen can be read by anyone passing, but a notebook kept private at home is safer.
exercise: «Real game companies never ask for your password on another website or in chat.» What is a password? | *A secret word or set of characters you type to use an account | A picture of your favourite game | A kind of computer screen | A nickname for a friend
exercise: «But her family had a password — a secret word only family and trusted friends know.» What is a password in this sentence? | A message sent by email | A song the family sings | A map of the house | *A secret word you say to be let into a place
exercise: Which of these is the safest password? | Ollie | your birthday | 123456 | *Sunny-Lake-Rocket7
test: «A sticky note on the screen can be read by anyone passing.» Where is a safer place to keep a password? | A sticky note on the screen | Written on a shared tablet | Shouted out in the playground | *A notebook kept somewhere private at home
test: Why are "Cat123" and "cat123" different passwords? | Numbers do not count | One is longer than the other | They look the same to a computer | *Capital letters count, so each letter must match exactly
exam: Ollie thinks someone knows his password. What should he do? | Tell nobody and keep using it | Write it on a sticky note | Share it with a friend to be fair | *Change it and tell an adult
